===Verb===
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|spell||ing-form}}
#* '''2006''', Wm. Shakespeare, Ann Thompson and Neil Taylor, eds., ''Hamlet'', London: Arden Shakespeare:
#*: [p 88] A persuasive theory about the authority of the quarto or Folio texts might shed light on how Shakespeare actually spelt these names in a particular manuscript, but, since Shakespeare seemed capable of '''spelling''' his own name differently on different occasions, how reliable a guide would such evidence be?

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}
<!-- (a verbal noun of the verb ''spell'') -->

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act, practice, ability, or subject of forming [[words]] with [[letters]], or of reading the letters of words; [[orthography]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904|author=w:Andrew Dickson White|title=Autobiography of Andrew Dickson White|page=43|passage=For the practical use of '''spelling''' comes in writing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=Henry Gallup Paine|title=Handbook of Simplified Spelling|location=New York|publisher=Simplified Spelling Board|page=1|passage='''Spelling''' was invented by man and, like other human inventions, is capable of development and improvement by man in the direction of simplicity, economy, and efficiency.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=[[w: Stephen White (author)|w: Stephen White]]|title=The Program|location=New York|publisher=Dell|page=66|passage=I knew that Kriciak, the inspector who was supervising me for the Marshals Service, was going to go nuts when I told him that I wanted to allow Landon to participate in soccer and '''spelling'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[manner]] of spelling of words; [[correct]] spelling.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=William Shakespeare|editor=Ann Thompson; Neil Taylor|title=Hamlet|location=London|publisher=Arden Shakespeare|page=88|passage=Because Elizabethan '''spelling''' was fluid, editors feel free to ‘modernize’ (correct) the '''spelling''' in the quartos and the Folio. But how is one to spell Rosencratz or Guildenstern, where the '''spelling''' varies, not only from text to text, but even within texts?}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[specific]] spelling of a word.
#* '''2006''', Wm. Shakespeare, Ann Thompson and Neil Taylor, eds., ''Hamlet'', London: Arden Shakespeare:
#*: [p 253] '''*excellent'''  Q2’s ‘extent’ is generally dismissed as an error, probably a mis-reading of ‘exlent’, a common '''spelling''' at this time.
#*: [p 269] ''reverend''  The '''spellings''' ‘reuerent’ (Q2) and ‘Reuerend’ (F) were interchangeable at this time.
#*: [p 466] Guildensterne and Rosincrance are F’s consistent '''spellings'''.
# {{lb|en|US|rare|dated|countable|or|uncountable}} A spelling [[test]] or [[spelling bee]].
#* '''1860''', [[w:Oscar Lawrence Jackson|Oscar Lawrence Jackson]], ''The Colonel's Diary: Journals Kept Before and During the Civil War'' [1922], Sharon, Penn., p 23:
#*: The boys were anxious for a '''spelling''' in the evening but I said no.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889|author=w:James Whitcomb Riley|chapter=A’ Old Played-Out Song|title=Pipes O' Pan at Zekesbury|location=Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher=Bowen-Merrill|page=45
|passage=How her face ust to look, in the twilight, / As I tuck her to '''spellin’'''; and she / Kep’ a-hummin’ that song ’tel I ast her, / Pine-blank, ef she ever missed me!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|editor=Carl Lindahl|title=American Folktales: From the Collections of the Library of Congress|volume=1|location=Armonk, N.Y.|publisher=M.E. Sharpe|page=416
|passage=So we'd sit with these girls during school hours, and we told them, if they'd slip off, that we'd get away, and we'd go to [the school] to a '''spelling'''.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A choice of [[notation]] among [[enharmonic]] equivalents for the same [[pitch]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Joshua Stoddard; Christopher Raphael; Paul E. Utgoff|url=https://archives.ismir.net/ismir2004/paper/000208.pdf|title=Well-Tempered Spelling: A Key-Invariant Pitch Spelling Algorithm|page=1
|passage=The '''spelling''' of a pitch is strongly influenced by its harmonic and melodic context.}}

